- Cross-site to Location A: PC11, PC13, PC14 → PC4 (172.16.10.4) all succeed with 0% packet loss.
- Location B (172.18.51.0/24 via router6): unreachable from Location C — confirmed due to router6's iptables FORWARD chain (drops all TCP/UDP/ICMP on inbound eth2), which is explicitly part of the baseline `/hostlab/router6.startup` security policy (ACL 110 translated from the original Cisco config), not an injected fault.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The network is fully operational and no fault is present. Router4 is up and healthy: its LAN-facing interface eth0 (192.168.200.1/24) is UP, both WAN links — eth4 to router5 (192.168.15.x) and eth5 to router3 (172.25.1.x) — are UP with 0% packet loss to their peers, and the RIP daemon has a complete routing table including all remote prefixes. Every Location C host (PC11–PC14) can reach the gateway at 192.168.200.1 with zero loss, and cross-site pings from PC11, PC13, and PC14 to Location A (PC4, 172.16.10.4) succeed with 0% loss across four hops.

The one connectivity limitation observed — Location C cannot reach Location B (172.18.50.0/24, 172.18.51.0/24 behind router6) — is caused by a pre-existing, deliberately configured iptables ACL on router6. The FORWARD chain drops all TCP, UDP, and ICMP arriving on router6's WAN interface eth2 (the r6_r3 link), permitting only a single TCP/80 flow from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1. This ACL is verbatim in router6's startup script as a translation of Cisco access-list 110 applied inbound on Serial2/0 — it is intentional security policy, not a fault.

The ticket's premise is incorrect: router4 has not failed, the default gateway responds normally, and inter-site routing to Location A works as expected. No corrective action is needed; the reported symptoms do not correspond to any actual network anomaly.
